text:
I love a green sea-beach;
 The roar of the waves is sweet;
 A rock or two, that break
 Upon the foam, that breaks;
 For me they need not be white.

Come over yonder, lass!
 There's a place for us,
 Come over yander lass!

The old churchyard, green with graves;
 The trees stand tall upon their stems;
 In the sunshine, on our knees we pray;
 We say, "Lord, bless this day!"

So when I reach my place on board the ship,
Where all things are prepared for evening's sleep;
O then do I forget each sad event,
Which in my bosom dwells so heavy bent;
Then I can sing with heart unrestrained,
I care not how the wind blows on the main;
For at such time as this it seems to me,
That though the earth rolls out her broad domains,
She is but one fair region where there reigns
Peace, joy, content, and innocence supreme

'Twas all in vain the mighty queen had tried
To gain the heart of a young soldier boy;
He never spoke one word nor looked her way,
But went from morn till night in search of prey;
She thought it was the common cause she saw
That made him fly from house to house so fast;
For then they said an old man lay at death
In each new village where the wolf was last.

The sun had risen long before they came;
But still its beams were on their path, for night
Had been so short in these hot climates,
That it was past midnight ere they left
Their beds at home, or put aside their sheets;
So here at last, beneath an open sky,

How did he fare in life’s long march through time?

With many a sorrow and much toil, and shame;
but when the days were done, came rest sublime.

At times he will feed on sweet milk from a gourd;

But more frequently at night he must be clothed

With sheepskins, and in them lie down without fire.

As the poet sings;
 As the singer speaks;
 So the song is sung:
 And so are words.

I think I have given all his pieces in English which appear under one title-page. He must be considered as having written only four pieces: the first two and the last above quoted, with an elegy on the death of Mr. Addison and another sonnet on the funeral of Mr.-Addison. This is the whole of the poetry he has left us (except for a few scraps) before we reach the age when the greater part of poets begin to write verse

When I was five years old I wanted to be an Emperor;
when I grew up I became one and had no time for playing Emperors;
I thought I would become rich then;
and when I had done so I found out there was more fun in spending money than making it;
then I thought I'd write books -- and now I find out that life is too short for being anything but myself.

In all that world I have not seen
 The beauty nor the grace of thee;
 Nor any living being
 As loving or as tenderly keen;
 None sweeter, none so glad as thou!
 My heart is wholly thine; my love is new.

I am but young, in years; the more for this,
 My heart can never see you in your pride,
 Your power without you, nor your greatness miss;
 And, sure 'tis little any man admires,
 Nor half so much if he were free from care
 That would displease him, till his head grow bare;
